# Conditions, switch expressions, and exhaustive decisions

## Branches encode partitions
An if/else chain should divide the input domain into understandable cases. Check boundaries before coding: an age of exactly 18 must belong to one branch, not fall into a gap. Use early returns to handle invalid input before the main calculation.

```java
int score = 70;
String result;
if (score < 0 || score > 100) {
    result = "invalid";
} else if (score >= 60) {
    result = "pass";
} else {
    result = "retry";
}
```
Changing >= to > changes the contract at 60. A ternary expression chooses one value: `score >= 60 ? "pass" : "retry"`. Nested ternaries quickly obscure multiple business rules.

## Switch expressions
```java
String action = "open";
int code = switch (action) {
    case "open" -> 1;
    case "close" -> 2;
    default -> -1;
};
```
An expression must produce a value for every allowed path. Arrow cases do not fall through. A multi-statement case block uses yield to return its value to the switch. Traditional colon-style switch statements can fall through without break. For a null selector, ordinary String cases and default do not provide a null guard; check first or use Java 21's explicit case null where appropriate.

## Practice
Build a shipping decision table using destination and weight. Include exact threshold weights and unknown destinations. Implement it with if first, then evaluate whether switch makes the choices clearer. Explain why a default branch may conceal a newly added enum case in later closed-domain models.

# Method contracts, parameters, return values, and side effects

## A method is a contract
A method declaration specifies a name, parameter types, return type, and body. Its useful contract additionally states valid inputs, side effects, and failure behavior. A return value can be composed by callers; printing inside a calculation couples it to the console.

```java
static int rectangleArea(int width, int height) {
    if (width < 0 || height < 0) {
        throw new IllegalArgumentException("negative dimension");
    }
    return Math.multiplyExact(width, height);
}
```
Place this member inside a class. The method accepts zero, rejects negatives, and reports overflow rather than wrapping. Every normally completing path must return an int. A void method has no return value, though it can return early.

## Arguments are copied
Java always passes arguments by value. Primitive values are copied. For an object argument, the copied value is a reference to the same object. A method can mutate an array through that reference, but reassigning its parameter to a new array does not reassign the caller's variable.

Local variables belong to each call. Static methods can be called without constructing an instance; later chapters distinguish them from instance behavior. Avoid using a mutable static field to pass results secretly.

## Practice
Separate a console calculator into reading, validating, calculating, and displaying. Test rectangleArea with (3,4), (0,4), negative dimensions, and overflow. Explain which two failure types are possible. Write one method that mutates an array and another that reassigns its array parameter, then predict the caller's observations.

# Overloading, varargs, argument passing, and recursion

## Overloads are selected at compilation
Two methods may share a name if their parameter lists differ. Return type alone cannot distinguish overloads. Selection uses the compile-time types of arguments and Java's conversion rules.
```java
static String label(int value) { return "int"; }
static String label(long value) { return "long"; }
// Within main:
System.out.println(label(2));  // int
System.out.println(label(2L)); // long
```
Widening, boxing, and varargs can make overload families surprising. Keep meanings consistent and avoid overloads that make null ambiguous.

## Varargs
`static int sum(int... values)` receives an int array. Callers may supply separate values, no values, or an existing array. Varargs must be last in the parameter list. Document whether null is allowed; zero arguments gives an empty array, not null.

## Recursion
```java
static int factorial(int n) {
    if (n < 0 || n > 12) throw new IllegalArgumentException();
    if (n == 0) return 1;
    return n * factorial(n - 1);
}
```
Each call has its own frame. The base case returns without another call; n-1 makes progress. The upper bound avoids int overflow. Java does not guarantee tail-call elimination, so deep recursion can exhaust the stack.

## Practice
Draw factorial(3) calls and returns: 3*2*1*1. Compare with an iterative version. Create int and long overloads and call them with a byte variable. Explain the selected method before compiling. Design a varargs sum whose empty result is zero and test that identity case.

# From examples and constraints to a decomposed algorithm

## Move from request to algorithm
Suppose a ledger must summarize valid positive expenses. Clarify whether refunds are excluded, how malformed data is reported, and whether totals can exceed int. Write examples: [300,200] -> 500; [] -> 0; [300,-50] -> 300 if refunds are excluded. These are specification choices, not implementation accidents.

## Separate decisions from transport
```java
static long positiveTotal(int[] amounts) {
    long total = 0;
    for (int amount : amounts) {
        if (amount > 0) total += amount;
    }
    return total;
}
```
The method does not read input or print. A caller can supply an array from a console, file, or test without changing the rule. Using long makes the accumulator wider than one int amount, but very large future input still needs a documented limit or checked addition.

## Decomposition strategy
Start with a direct correct algorithm. Name transformations after their meaning: parseAmount, validateTransaction, positiveTotal, formatSummary. Avoid dividing code merely every ten lines; cohesive steps have understandable inputs and outputs. A boolean flag that changes a method into two unrelated operations often suggests two names instead.

## Practice
Design a menu-driven expense report. Write pseudocode before Java. Identify which functions are deterministic and which touch input/output. Add a requirement to ignore expenses below a threshold and trace the boundary equal to that threshold. Explain how to change the calculation without changing the console loop. Keep a test for every clarified business rule.

